Chapter: CellCsv
  cites Sheets chapter CellStore
  cites Foreword chapter CCE

 CSV in and out, to RFC 4180: fields separated by commas, records by CRLF, a
 field optionally wrapped in double quotes, and inside a quoted field a pair
 of double quotes standing for one literal quote. An unquoted field carries
 no comma, no quote and no line ending.

 The scan runs over UTF-8 BYTES rather than CCE code points, as `Http.codex`
 parses a request. Every byte of a multi-byte UTF-8 sequence has its high bit
 set, so no character outside ASCII can be mistaken for a comma, a quote or a
 line ending, and such a character's bytes pass into the field untouched.

 What a field becomes, and the rules are the reason the store grew a kind:

 - an UNQUOTED empty field becomes an EMPTY cell, which is the distinction
   `a,,b` exists to make and which a store of bare numbers cannot hold;
 - an UNQUOTED field of ASCII digits, with an optional leading minus,
   becomes a NUMBER cell;
 - a QUOTED field is ALWAYS text, because quoting is how a CSV says its
   content is not to be read as a quantity;
 - everything else becomes a TEXT cell.

 Two cases are not round trips and the arm records both. A number is written
 back as `show` writes it, so `007` in is `7` out. And a text cell holding
 the empty string writes as nothing, which reads back as an empty cell,
 because CSV has no spelling that separates the two.

 We say:

Section: The Bytes That Matter

  csv-comma : Integer = 44
  csv-quote : Integer = 34
  csv-cr : Integer = 13
  csv-lf : Integer = 10
  csv-minus : Integer = 45
  csv-zero : Integer = 48
  csv-nine : Integer = 57

  csv-eol : Text
  csv-eol = utf8-bytes-to-text [13, 10]

  csv-sep : Text
  csv-sep = utf8-bytes-to-text [44]

Section: Reading A Field

  csv-digits : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  csv-digits (fld) (i) (n) =
    if i >= n then True
    else if list-at fld i < csv-zero then False
    else if list-at fld i > csv-nine then False
    else csv-digits fld (i + 1) n

  csv-numeric : List Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  csv-numeric (fld) (n) =
    if n == 0 then False
    else if list-at fld 0 /= csv-minus then csv-digits fld 0 n
    else if n == 1 then False
    else csv-digits fld 1 n

  csv-acc : List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  csv-acc (fld) (i) (n) (a) =
    if i >= n then a
    else csv-acc fld (i + 1) n (a * 10 + (list-at fld i - csv-zero))

  csv-value : List Integer, Integer -> Integer
  csv-value (fld) (n) =
    if list-at fld 0 == csv-minus then 0 - csv-acc fld 1 n 0
    else csv-acc fld 0 n 0

 A quoted field is text whatever it looks like, which is why `quoted` is
 carried through the scan rather than inferred from the bytes at the end.

  csv-emit : Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer, Boolean -> Boolean
  csv-emit (blk) (r) (c) (fld) (quoted) =
    let n = list-length fld
    in if quoted then sheet-set-text blk r c (utf8-bytes-to-text fld)
    else if n == 0 then sheet-clear blk r c
    else if csv-numeric fld n then sheet-set blk r c (csv-value fld n)
    else sheet-set-text blk r c (utf8-bytes-to-text fld)

Section: Reading A Sheet

  csv-finish : Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer, Boolean -> Integer
  csv-finish (blk) (r) (c) (fld) (quoted) =
    if list-length fld > 0 then csv-finish-emit blk r c fld quoted
    else if c > 0 then csv-finish-emit blk r c fld quoted
    else if quoted then csv-finish-emit blk r c fld quoted
    else r

  csv-finish-emit : Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer, Boolean -> Integer
  csv-finish-emit (blk) (r) (c) (fld) (quoted) =
    let e = csv-emit blk r c fld quoted
    in r + 1

  csv-scan : Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer, Boolean, Boolean -> Integer
  csv-scan (blk) (bs) (n) (i) (r) (c) (fld) (inq) (quoted) =
    if i >= n then csv-finish blk r c fld quoted
    else if inq then csv-scan-quoted blk bs n i r c fld quoted
    else csv-scan-plain blk bs n i r c fld quoted

  csv-scan-quoted : Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer, Boolean -> Integer
  csv-scan-quoted (blk) (bs) (n) (i) (r) (c) (fld) (quoted) =
    let b = list-at bs i
    in if b /= csv-quote then csv-scan blk bs n (i + 1) r c (list-push fld b) True True
    else if i + 1 >= n then csv-scan blk bs n (i + 1) r c fld False True
    else if list-at bs (i + 1) == csv-quote then csv-scan blk bs n (i + 2) r c (list-push fld csv-quote) True True
    else csv-scan blk bs n (i + 1) r c fld False True

 A carriage return outside a quoted field is dropped rather than kept: a CSV
 written on either kind of machine ends its records with LF, with or without
 a CR in front, and a CR left in the field would ride into the cell.

  csv-scan-plain : Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer, Boolean -> Integer
  csv-scan-plain (blk) (bs) (n) (i) (r) (c) (fld) (quoted) =
    let b = list-at bs i
    in if b == csv-quote then csv-scan blk bs n (i + 1) r c fld True True
    else if b == csv-comma then csv-next-field blk bs n i r c fld quoted
    else if b == csv-lf then csv-next-record blk bs n i r c fld quoted
    else if b == csv-cr then csv-scan blk bs n (i + 1) r c fld False quoted
    else csv-scan blk bs n (i + 1) r c (list-push fld b) False quoted

  csv-next-field : Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer, Boolean -> Integer
  csv-next-field (blk) (bs) (n) (i) (r) (c) (fld) (quoted) =
    let e = csv-emit blk r c fld quoted
    in csv-scan blk bs n (i + 1) r (c + 1) [] False False

  csv-next-record : Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer, Boolean -> Integer
  csv-next-record (blk) (bs) (n) (i) (r) (c) (fld) (quoted) =
    let e = csv-emit blk r c fld quoted
    in csv-scan blk bs n (i + 1) (r + 1) 0 [] False False

 The answer is the number of RECORDS read, which is what a caller checks
 against the sheet's row count to learn that the sheet was too small: a field
 past the bounds is refused by the store and silently absent otherwise.

  csv-load : Integer, Text -> Integer
  csv-load (blk) (t) =
    let bs = text-to-utf8-bytes t
    in csv-scan blk bs (list-length bs) 0 0 0 [] False False

Section: Writing

  csv-needs-quote : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  csv-needs-quote (bs) (i) (n) =
    if i >= n then False
    else if list-at bs i == csv-comma then True
    else if list-at bs i == csv-quote then True
    else if list-at bs i == csv-cr then True
    else if list-at bs i == csv-lf then True
    else csv-needs-quote bs (i + 1) n

  csv-escape : List Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  csv-escape (bs) (i) (n) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else if list-at bs i == csv-quote then csv-escape bs (i + 1) n (list-push (list-push acc csv-quote) csv-quote)
    else csv-escape bs (i + 1) n (list-push acc (list-at bs i))

 A text cell whose content would READ BACK as a number is quoted even though
 RFC 4180 does not require it, because the alternative is a round trip that
 silently changes a cell's kind: a text cell holding `42` written bare is a
 number cell when the file is read again.

  csv-wrap : Text -> Text
  csv-wrap (t) =
    let bs = text-to-utf8-bytes t
    in let n = list-length bs
    in if csv-needs-quote bs 0 n then csv-quote-it bs n
    else if csv-numeric bs n then csv-quote-it bs n
    else t

  csv-quote-it : List Integer, Integer -> Text
  csv-quote-it (bs) (n) =
    utf8-bytes-to-text (list-push (csv-escape bs 0 n (list-push [] csv-quote)) csv-quote)

  csv-field : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  csv-field (blk) (r) (c) =
    let k = sheet-kind blk r c
    in if k == cs-number then show (sheet-get blk r c)
    else if k == cs-text then csv-wrap (sheet-text blk r c)
    else ""

  csv-row : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  csv-row (blk) (r) (c) (cols) (acc) =
    if c >= cols then acc
    else if c == 0 then csv-row blk r 1 cols (csv-field blk r 0)
    else csv-row blk r (c + 1) cols (acc & csv-sep & csv-field blk r c)

  csv-rows : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  csv-rows (blk) (r) (n) (cols) (acc) =
    if r >= n then acc
    else csv-rows blk (r + 1) n cols (acc & csv-row blk r 0 cols "" & csv-eol)

 `csv-save` is told how many records and how many columns to write rather
 than reading the sheet's own bounds, because a sheet is allocated at its
 maximum: writing every row and column of a 1,000 by 26 sheet holding four
 cells emits a thousand records of twenty-five commas.

  csv-save : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  csv-save (blk) (rows) (cols) =
    let n = if rows > sheet-rows blk then sheet-rows blk else rows
    in let w = if cols > sheet-cols blk then sheet-cols blk else cols
    in csv-rows blk 0 n w ""

